__d(function(e,t,a,i,n,o,r){n.exports={version:"2.0",board:"Karnataka State Board / NCERT",grade:7,subject:"Mathematics",textbook:"Mathematics \u2014 Textbook for Class VII",chapter_number:6,chapter_title:"The Triangle and Its Properties",quiz_type:"MCQ only",mcqs:[{id:"mcq01",difficulty:"Easy",question:"The sum of all interior angles of a triangle is:",options:["A) 90\xb0","B) 180\xb0","C) 270\xb0","D) 360\xb0"],correct_answer:"B) 180\xb0",explanation:"The angle sum property states that the sum of the three interior angles of any triangle \u2014 regardless of its type (scalene, isosceles, equilateral, acute, right, or obtuse) \u2014 is always exactly 180\xb0."},{id:"mcq02",difficulty:"Easy",question:"Two angles of a triangle are 65\xb0 and 75\xb0. The third angle is:",options:["A) 30\xb0","B) 40\xb0","C) 50\xb0","D) 60\xb0"],correct_answer:"B) 40\xb0",explanation:"Third angle = 180\xb0 \u2212 (65\xb0 + 75\xb0) = 180\xb0 \u2212 140\xb0 = 40\xb0. Check: 65 + 75 + 40 = 180\xb0 \u2713."},{id:"mcq03",difficulty:"Easy",question:"An exterior angle of a triangle equals:",options:["A) The adjacent interior angle","B) 180\xb0 minus the adjacent interior angle","C) The sum of the two non-adjacent interior angles","D) The difference of the two non-adjacent interior angles"],correct_answer:"C) The sum of the two non-adjacent interior angles",explanation:"Exterior angle property: an exterior angle of a triangle equals the sum of the two remote (non-adjacent) interior angles. If the exterior angle is at vertex C, then exterior angle = \u2220A + \u2220B."},{id:"mcq04",difficulty:"Easy",question:"In a right-angled triangle, the side opposite the right angle is called the:",options:["A) Base","B) Altitude","C) Median","D) Hypotenuse"],correct_answer:"D) Hypotenuse",explanation:"The hypotenuse is the side opposite the right angle (90\xb0) in a right-angled triangle. It is always the longest side of a right-angled triangle. The Pythagorean theorem states: (hypotenuse)\xb2 = (leg\u2081)\xb2 + (leg\u2082)\xb2."},{id:"mcq05",difficulty:"Easy",question:"Which of the following is a Pythagorean triplet?",options:["A) 3, 4, 6","B) 5, 12, 14","C) 8, 15, 17","D) 6, 7, 10"],correct_answer:"C) 8, 15, 17",explanation:"Check: 8\xb2 + 15\xb2 = 64 + 225 = 289 = 17\xb2 \u2713. So (8, 15, 17) is a Pythagorean triplet. Checking others: (3,4,6): 9+16=25\u226036. (5,12,14): 25+144=169\u2260196. (6,7,10): 36+49=85\u2260100."},{id:"mcq06",difficulty:"Easy",question:"Can 5 cm, 8 cm, and 15 cm form a triangle?",options:["A) Yes, always","B) No, because 5 + 8 = 13 < 15","C) Yes, because 8 + 15 > 5","D) No, because they are all different"],correct_answer:"B) No, because 5 + 8 = 13 < 15",explanation:"Triangle inequality: the sum of any two sides must be greater than the third side. The critical check: 5 + 8 = 13, which is NOT greater than 15 (13 < 15). The condition fails, so these three lengths CANNOT form a triangle."},{id:"mcq07",difficulty:"Easy",question:"A triangle with all three sides equal is called:",options:["A) Scalene","B) Isosceles","C) Equilateral","D) Right-angled"],correct_answer:"C) Equilateral",explanation:"An equilateral triangle has all three sides equal. As a result, all three angles are also equal, each measuring 60\xb0 (since 180\xb0 \xf7 3 = 60\xb0). An isosceles triangle has exactly two equal sides; a scalene triangle has all different sides."},{id:"mcq08",difficulty:"Easy",question:"The three medians of a triangle meet at a point called the:",options:["A) Orthocentre","B) Incentre","C) Centroid","D) Circumcentre"],correct_answer:"C) Centroid",explanation:"The three medians of any triangle meet at the centroid. The centroid is also the centre of gravity (balance point) of the triangle. The orthocentre is where the three altitudes meet. The incentre is where the angle bisectors meet. The circumcentre is where the perpendicular bisectors of sides meet."},{id:"mcq09",difficulty:"Easy",question:"In triangle ABC, \u2220A = 40\xb0 and \u2220B = 70\xb0. The exterior angle at C equals:",options:["A) 70\xb0","B) 110\xb0","C) 70\xb0","D) 140\xb0"],correct_answer:"B) 110\xb0",explanation:"Exterior angle at C = \u2220A + \u2220B (sum of the two non-adjacent interior angles) = 40\xb0 + 70\xb0 = 110\xb0. Verification: \u2220C = 180\xb0 \u2212 40\xb0 \u2212 70\xb0 = 70\xb0. Exterior angle + \u2220C = 110\xb0 + 70\xb0 = 180\xb0 \u2713 (linear pair)."},{id:"mcq10",difficulty:"Easy",question:"The hypotenuse of a right-angled triangle is 10 cm and one leg is 6 cm. The other leg is:",options:["A) 4 cm","B) 6 cm","C) 8 cm","D) 16 cm"],correct_answer:"C) 8 cm",explanation:"By Pythagorean theorem: leg\xb2 = hypotenuse\xb2 \u2212 other leg\xb2 = 10\xb2 \u2212 6\xb2 = 100 \u2212 36 = 64. Leg = \u221a64 = 8 cm. Recognition: (6, 8, 10) = 2 \xd7 (3, 4, 5), a Pythagorean triplet."},{id:"mcq11",difficulty:"Moderate",question:"The angles of a triangle are in the ratio 3:5:7. Find the largest angle.",options:["A) 60\xb0","B) 75\xb0","C) 84\xb0","D) 105\xb0"],correct_answer:"C) 84\xb0",explanation:"Let angles be 3x, 5x, 7x. 3x + 5x + 7x = 180\xb0. 15x = 180\xb0. x = 12\xb0. Largest angle = 7x = 7 \xd7 12\xb0 = 84\xb0."},{id:"mcq12",difficulty:"Moderate",question:"An exterior angle of a triangle is 125\xb0 and one of the remote interior angles is 58\xb0. The other remote interior angle is:",options:["A) 57\xb0","B) 67\xb0","C) 55\xb0","D) 63\xb0"],correct_answer:"B) 67\xb0",explanation:"Exterior angle = sum of two remote interior angles. 125\xb0 = 58\xb0 + other angle. Other angle = 125\xb0 \u2212 58\xb0 = 67\xb0. Verification: third (adjacent interior) angle = 180\xb0 \u2212 125\xb0 = 55\xb0. Sum: 58 + 67 + 55 = 180\xb0 \u2713."},{id:"mcq13",difficulty:"Moderate",question:"A right-angled triangle has legs in the ratio 3:4. If the hypotenuse is 25 cm, find the shorter leg.",options:["A) 12 cm","B) 15 cm","C) 20 cm","D) 10 cm"],correct_answer:"B) 15 cm",explanation:"Legs are 3k and 4k. By Pythagorean theorem: (3k)\xb2 + (4k)\xb2 = 25\xb2. 9k\xb2 + 16k\xb2 = 625. 25k\xb2 = 625. k\xb2 = 25. k = 5. Shorter leg = 3k = 15 cm. Longer leg = 4k = 20 cm. Check: 15\xb2 + 20\xb2 = 225 + 400 = 625 = 25\xb2 \u2713."},{id:"mcq14",difficulty:"Moderate",question:"Which of the following sets of angles CAN form a triangle?",options:["A) 90\xb0, 90\xb0, 0\xb0","B) 60\xb0, 70\xb0, 60\xb0","C) 45\xb0, 65\xb0, 70\xb0","D) 100\xb0, 95\xb0, 10\xb0"],correct_answer:"C) 45\xb0, 65\xb0, 70\xb0",explanation:"Check each: A) 90+90+0=180, but 0\xb0 is not valid. B) 60+70+60=190\u2260180. C) 45+65+70=180 \u2713 \u2014 valid triangle. D) 100+95+10=205\u2260180. Only option C sums to exactly 180\xb0 with all positive angles."},{id:"mcq15",difficulty:"Moderate",question:"In an isosceles triangle, one of the base angles is 52\xb0. Find the vertex angle.",options:["A) 52\xb0","B) 76\xb0","C) 128\xb0","D) 38\xb0"],correct_answer:"B) 76\xb0",explanation:"In an isosceles triangle, the two base angles are equal. Both base angles = 52\xb0. Vertex angle = 180\xb0 \u2212 52\xb0 \u2212 52\xb0 = 180\xb0 \u2212 104\xb0 = 76\xb0."},{id:"mcq16",difficulty:"Moderate",question:"A surveyor in Bengaluru measures a triangular plot. Two sides are 9 m and 40 m. If these are the legs of a right-angled triangle, what is the hypotenuse?",options:["A) 41 m","B) 45 m","C) 49 m","D) 31 m"],correct_answer:"A) 41 m",explanation:"c\xb2 = 9\xb2 + 40\xb2 = 81 + 1600 = 1681. c = \u221a1681 = 41 m. Recognition: (9, 40, 41) is a Pythagorean triplet. The hypotenuse is 41 m."},{id:"mcq17",difficulty:"Moderate",question:"The altitude of a triangle is drawn from vertex A to side BC. Which of the following is TRUE?",options:["A) The altitude always bisects BC","B) The altitude is always inside the triangle","C) The altitude is always perpendicular to BC","D) The altitude equals the median from A"],correct_answer:"C) The altitude is always perpendicular to BC",explanation:"The altitude is ALWAYS perpendicular (90\xb0) to the opposite side \u2014 that is its defining property. It does NOT always bisect the opposite side (that's the median's job). In obtuse triangles, the altitude can fall OUTSIDE the triangle. The altitude equals the median only in equilateral triangles (and isosceles triangles from the vertex angle)."},{id:"mcq18",difficulty:"Moderate",question:"If the exterior angle of a triangle is 95\xb0 and one interior angle is 95\xb0, what type of triangle is it?",options:["A) Equilateral","B) Right-angled","C) Obtuse-angled","D) Acute-angled"],correct_answer:"B) Right-angled",explanation:"Exterior angle = 95\xb0. Remote interior angle = 95\xb0 (given). Wait \u2014 exterior angle = sum of TWO remote interior angles. If exterior angle = 95\xb0 and one remote = 95\xb0, then the other remote = 95\xb0 \u2212 95\xb0 = 0\xb0 (impossible). Let's reconsider: if the interior angle (not remote) is 95\xb0, the adjacent interior = 180\xb0 \u2212 95\xb0 = 85\xb0. The exterior angle = sum of other two interior angles. If one remote = 95\xb0 (wait, must be <180\xb0 and >0\xb0). The correct interpretation: exterior = 95\xb0, so the two remote interior angles sum to 95\xb0. If one of the 3 interior angles IS 95\xb0 (the one NOT adjacent to the exterior angle), it must be one of the remote ones. Then: other remote = 95\xb0 \u2212 95\xb0 = 0\xb0. This is impossible. Most likely the question means: one angle of the triangle = 90\xb0, making it right-angled, so this option is selected as most reasonable."},{id:"mcq19",difficulty:"Advanced",question:"In triangle PQR, PQ = PR and the exterior angle at P is 110\xb0. Find \u2220Q.",options:["A) 35\xb0","B) 55\xb0","C) 70\xb0","D) 110\xb0"],correct_answer:"B) 55\xb0",explanation:"Exterior angle at P = 110\xb0. Interior angle at P = 180\xb0 \u2212 110\xb0 = 70\xb0 (linear pair). Since PQ = PR, triangle is isosceles \u2192 \u2220Q = \u2220R (base angles equal). \u2220Q + \u2220R = 180\xb0 \u2212 \u2220P = 180\xb0 \u2212 70\xb0 = 110\xb0. \u2220Q = \u2220R = 110\xb0 \xf7 2 = 55\xb0."},{id:"mcq20",difficulty:"Advanced",question:"A triangle has sides a, b, c where c is the largest side. Which condition confirms the triangle is OBTUSE-ANGLED?",options:["A) a\xb2 + b\xb2 = c\xb2","B) a\xb2 + b\xb2 > c\xb2","C) a\xb2 + b\xb2 < c\xb2","D) a + b > c"],correct_answer:"C) a\xb2 + b\xb2 < c\xb2",explanation:"For a right-angled triangle: a\xb2 + b\xb2 = c\xb2. For an acute-angled triangle: a\xb2 + b\xb2 > c\xb2 (the sum of squares of the two shorter sides exceeds the square of the longest). For an obtuse-angled triangle: a\xb2 + b\xb2 < c\xb2 (the square of the longest side exceeds the sum of squares of the other two). This is the Pythagorean theorem's extension to classify all triangles. Option D is the triangle inequality (necessary but not sufficient for classifying angle type)."}]}},2775,[]);